cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or paper, please cite it as below."
title: "Realized-Volatility Forecasting and Volatility-Managed Sizing on the S&P 500: A Pre-Registered, Walk-Forward, Multiple-Testing-Corrected Study"
authors:
  - family-names: "Löffler"
    given-names: "Jonas"
date-released: "2026-08-31"
version: "1.0"
type: software
keywords:
  - realized volatility
  - HAR model
  - volatility-managed portfolios
  - VIX term structure
  - walk-forward analysis
  - backtest overfitting
  - deflated Sharpe ratio
  - null result
license: MIT
abstract: >
  A pre-registered, walk-forward, multiple-testing-corrected study on 21 years of
  1-minute S&P 500 data (plus the VIX term structure). The forecasting result
  replicates cleanly: log-HAR beats the random walk out-of-sample under QLIKE
  (Clark-West p approx 0.03), implied volatility adds information (HAR-IV), and a
  rich linear feature set edges HAR-IV through features rather than regularization
  or nonlinearity; a loss-matched Gamma-GLM with the VIX term-structure slope is the
  one forecasting increment beyond replication (lower QLIKE and MSE, DM p = 0.0008).
  A lag diagnostic shows the forecast's residual "lag" is a 2.5x underestimation of
  the worst volatility spikes that an intraday nowcast only partly reduces. The
  trading result is reported as a NULL: the best walk-forward
  volatility-managed / VIX-term-structure combination (Sharpe 0.96, max drawdown
  -9.9 percent) is disqualified by the study's own pre-registered rules (probability
  of backtest overfitting 0.84; single-market effect), its Sharpe advantage over
  buy-and-hold is inside the bootstrap noise band, and its clean drawdown profile is
  largely a volatility-level artifact. All failures are reported. No result reaches
  supported status without a live, ex-ante prediction log.
